>I don't think there is anything wrong with reading users' .newsrc
>files to create the monthly arbitron statistics provided that users
>are given notice.
>
>Question: Should users be allowed to opt out (without having
>to give up reading Netnews altogether)?

I see no real point in providing this option.  The arbitron program
does not say "Users X, Y and Z  read comp.foo.baz;" rather, it says,
"3 users are subscribed to comp.foo.baz."  Given that the program 
does not link individual users to the groups they read, I fail to
see a "privacy problem."

Consider, too, that a site where a significant number of users "opt
out" might as well forget about running arbitron - skewed stats,
you know..."Oh, nobody reads alt.drugs?  OK, off it goes..." 

Many sites use arbitron data to determine expiration times for news-
groups; the more popular groups are given longer expiration times.
Some sites also use the local readership info to decide if groups
can be deleted (or have their expiration time reduced).  

I just don't see this as a problem.  

The one exception might be a smaller site, where the number of 
readers is so small that individual user preferences could be 
ascertained by the process of elimination.  ("Oh, 5 people read 
alt.sex.woonga?  Hmm..we only have 8 people reading news at all!  
Hmm...Bill?  Steve?...)  Those sites would be better off not run-
ning arbitron at all.

--Wes

