I can't see where there is much to do about this particular incident. The
student signed a contract saying he would not abuse his account privlidges (I
will try to get a copy of this) and then (as determined by UF sysadmins and,
frankly, many of us agree at least in part) violating it. 

Suspending an account is not denying education or the right to speak, although
it may (does, for me) place an undue burden on free association with one's
peers. Because net access is not universal (and individuals are only allowed
access by the willingness of the provider and, usually, some kind of contract)
I can't see how this is really a First Amendment issue. 

The real problem here is the contract in the first place; ours here basically
says "If you aren't using your account for legitimate classwork, we can throw
you out." It is so vague that I can't tell if reading news is appropriate use
(since I have yet to have a professor assign it as homework.) It mentions
email spam, but not news spam and I don't know if posting articles in certain
(correctly on-topic) groups could be interpreted as inappropriate use of
university computers for "supporting a particular political or religious
viewpoint." (That is not verbatim; I will find the original.) 

In practice, news is just fine (and we have alt.*) but as far as I can tell,
the only definition of inappropriate use is "I know it when I see it." 

:
:Spamming is NOT free speech, any more than shouting down a speaker you
:do not like is free speech. It is an abuse of net access and a breach
:of the spirit of cooperation and personal responsibility which makes
:the Internet and Usenet possible.
:
Cooperation is not backed up by laws, and, as many people have pointed out,
when people don't voluntarily cooperate, it falls apart. We may need someone
to define what is right and wrong in no uncertain terms, but I don't think I
want to be around when that happens. 

