    It's a first amendment issue if the account is being suspended
    because of content in a message.  And the spammer could argue that
    that is in fact the case here.  Remember, if the provider is an arm of
    the US government, the first amendment takes precedence over contract
    law - it cannot enter into a contract that is in violation of the first
    amendment. 

    Case law is fairly undeveloped as to first amendment interpretation for
    conferencing/bulletin board/usenet access, but there is other law which
    might be applicable here.  (EFF has oodles of background material on
    this subject.)  For example, a public US University may choose to set
    up a student newspaper as a forum for student communication, but they
    may not shut down the newspaper - once established - if they decide
    they dislike what is being said in it.  It *might* extend that a public
    US University may choose whether or not to grant student computer
    accounts with internet access, but may not revoke such access - once
    established - because they do not like what is being said even if that
    speech violates norms of decorum or netiquette guidelines.

    But this is just theory - to my knowledge it has not been tested in the
    courts.  (Does anyone know otherwise?)

