Consider this question from the perspective that many sites cannot 
carry all available newsgroups.  How, then, should the site decide
upon the deletion/expiry-extension of groups?  Quite frankly, it's
"overkill" to convene public discussion on each and every change in
one's newsfeed.  Arbitron provides the best means of estimating the
local readership of one's newsfeed; as far as I can see, there's no
more equitable means of gauging user interest.  (Consider, too, the
fact that readers of 'controversial' groups are not always likely
to engage in public debate; arbitron provides a relatively anony-
mous means by which their interest is considered.)

If I can wipe/one-day-expire a high-volume group because *no one* is 
subscribed to it, it's important; that makes room for either additional 
newsgroups or longer expirations on popular groups.  In my opinion, it 
*is* that important.

