  I really have an objection to this.  While I oppose any legal action be 
taken to limit spamming, I do not believe that charters and netiquette are 
simply suggestions.  How many times have unruly people been removed from a 
public forum for disrupting normal proceedings?  If a political speaker were 
to come to a university (or ANY public place) and start with his/her speech, 
and someone in the audience were to stand up and start shouting at the 
speaker, it would not be long at all before that person is ushered out of the 
place.  Yet, other than "disturbing the peace" or some such minor law, there 
is nothing this person did wrong other than disrupting normal activity.  If 
that can happen in the real world and be accepted as a correct practice, then 
removing a user from Usenet for spamming should also be an accepted practice.  
That is what netiquette is intended to provide for.

  How do charters?  Charters are analogous to posting an announcement on the 
front door of the public forum stating what will be discussed inside that 
forum.  For example, "COME ON IN!  We will be talking about the blue whale and 
why it is becomming extinct."  If you don't like that topic, you wouldn't 
attend.  If you do go in and start talking about how microwaves are causing 
cancer in cows, you will be asked to refrain from that discussion because it 
is not appropriate for that forum.  If you continue, you will be asked to 
leave.  If you still continue, you will be removed, or just ignored.  This is 
not spamming in this case, but the etiquette of the forum, established 
partially by the posted topic on the door, forbids you from discussing 
microwaves in that forum, else you will be dealt with accordingly.

  The idea that "if it isn't against the law, you are free to do it" is 
absurd.  If you go into a public forum, decide you don't like the speaker, and 
cut the wire from the microphone to the wall, you have broken no law.  But 
see just how long you would remian in that forum afterward.  You 
wouldn't do it because it is not an accepted practice in society.

  So while I agree with you that no laws should forbid spamming, I disagree 
that netiquette and charters are mere suggestions.  Rather they are accepted 
social norms necessary for any social group to survive.  If you allow spammers 
to go unchecked, they will end up choking Usenet into nonexistence.

Just my opinion...which happens to agree with lots of others'.

