I don't know whether your university is public or private.  (If you're at
University of Arizona, I believe that's a state school.  Of course, the
presdient there has a poor track record when it come to protecting speech...)

In terms of the First Amendment, precedent opposes the idea that a government
agent can arbitrarily confine expression to a speech ghetto.  See, for
example, the discussion of time, place and manner restrictions and
traditional public forums in Perry v. Perry (1983).

Reasonable time, place an manner restrictions can, of course, be implemented.
 Consequently, it would be perfectly legal for a public institution to adopt
a rule restricting expression in classrooms, in order to facilitate the use
of those classrooms for their intended purpose.

It would not, on the other hand, be legal for a public institution to
arbitrarily divide a commons into a "free speech" region and a "not free
speech" region.  A legitimate time, place and manner restriction must
narrowly-drawn to serve a significant government interest.  Protecting
individuals from offense in a traditional public forum (and even in many
other places) has never been considered a significant government interest.


