That is an interesting analogy, Carl.

Spamming is not quite the same thing as amplification, though.  Amplification
is a problem because the music, speeches, etc., do not remain within the
public forum.  They instead spill over into private offices, etc.  A spammed
message does not leave the public forum (through the act of spamming itself).
It goes right where the sender wants it to go.  It only goes somewhere else if
someone forwards the message.

I also do not know how you could write a restriction that is clear and narrow
and could not be used by intolerant readers to charge "spamming" in order to
silence someone.

Messages about repenting, while they may seem off-topic to some (and annoying
as hell to me), may not be off-topic to others.  The spammer could argue she
was making a public service announcement.  Nothing more.  She could claim,
quite credibly, she was concerned about the welfare of others.  Disruption
could be the furthest thing from her mind.

That is the major problem with this sort of rule.  You have to be able to sort
out serious intentions from frivilous intentions.  As one who has been falsely
accused many times in misc.writing and rec.pets.cats of frivilous intentions
(i.e., a desire to disrupt, a desire to destroy the nesgroup, etc.), I can see
this sort of rule being used by intolerant readers to suppress messages and
authors they find personally annoying.


Mitch

