My understanding is that their are two times in which a government
agent can regulate, for example, amplification.

  The first is when that amplification substantially disrupts some one
  else. In such a case, the government can regulate the amplification
  even on private property. For example, if an private apartment near
  campus had such loud amplification that it disrupted a nearby class,
  the university could call the police and the police could compel the
  apartment dweller to end to the disruption.

  The second is when the amplification is in a created state-owned
  forum. In such a case, the government is allowed to impose
  "reasonable" (content-neutral) time, place, and manner restrictions.
  Note that this is a much easier standard for the government. The
  government doesn't have to show that the regulation is necessary to
  stop substantial disruption. It only has to show that the regulation
  is "reasonable". I don't know the case law, but I imagine that a
  reasonable rule might be: Without special authorization from the
  Dean of Facilities, you may not use amplifiers over 25 watts on the
  Quad.

If this characterization is correct, the question is this:
 Would a rule like:

  "Without special authorization from Computer Services, users may not
  posting identical or nearly identical articles to more than 25
  newsgroups in any twenty-four hour period."

 be considered "reasonable"?

I think it would be.

