I see no reason why I should be bound by your original decisions as to
where my replies to the thread belong.  If I decide that my reply should
solely be in rec.pet.cats I may well reach far fewer people with my reply
than if I sent it to both groups, but that's my problem, not yours. 

A thread in an un-moderated newsgroup is exactly the same as a free form
discussion in a roomful of people.  You may say something to a group in
your room in a loud enough voice so that I can hear it in another room. 
How many rooms you reach is a function of how loud you holler, exactly the
same as cross posting to many newsgroups.  I may well decide to make a
reply to your statement that is audible only to those in the same room
with me, or I may do the same as you and make my reply loud enough so that
both rooms, or more, can hear me.  The choice is solely mine and in no way
governed by your wishes or desires as to how many people should be hearing
the conversation.  I may not reach the same number of people as you do,
but then that is my decision to make, not yours. 

A thread, like a free form oral discussion, has two or more sides and the
form it follows is governed by ALL those participating in it, not solely
by the one who originated it.  It can stay as one large discussion with
everybody participating, or it can break down into smaller groups of which
you may, or may not, also be a participant. 

In short, only I have the right to decide where my replies shall go in
un-moderated newsgroups.  My reply might end up in an in-appropriate
newsgroup but that is my decision to make.  Saying anything else is the
same as wishful thinking. 

This in no way can be construed as trying to move your replies to where
you don't want them to go.  You have the same ability as I do to adjust
the header addresses to those of your choosing. 

You have the right to add the original follow-up newsgroups back in if you
choose to answer my reply in the newsgroup I put it in.  That's the only 
right you have in the matter.

Paul G. Stout

