Here's my favorite definition.  I do not claim it to be the only correct 
definition, although I do believe it belongs to the set of good definitions.
I didn't come up with it myself, but I cannot remember who did.

Spamming in the context of USENET:  Individually posting (as opposed to 
crossposting) substantially identical articles to more then the square 
root of the total number of newsgroups carried by your site, over a 
period of time such that at least this number of copies are simultaneously 
resident on the server.  Thus, it is easier to spam a small site, and 
harder to spam a site with a short expire time. 

This is meant to reflect that the real effect of spamming that I wish to 
avoid is not the irritation (I could live without that, too).  Rather, it 
is the inordinate use of storage in the news server.  Crossposting only 
leaves one copy of the article resident, while individually posting N 
articles results in N copies resident on the server.

There may be well-defined and accepted definitions based on other metrics 
as well.  One "consensus" definition seems to be 

Spamming:  Individually posting a large number of substantially identical 
articles, mostly to newsgroups for which the article is off-topic.  
Crying censorship when people in those off-topic newsgroups complain to 
your postmaster, usually on the basis that your message is important to 
all humanity, and USENET (in general) is a public forum, part of the 
information superhighway which the government built for the good of the 
people of the united states.  

The refutation of the last three assertions is left as an exercise to the 
interested student.  There will be a quiz next class. :-)

