My thoughts on this topic are evolving, too, Danny.  Spamming is a novelty
in the area of censorship.

I don't think the social contract is that you can place one copy of your
message in my space in return for my right to place one copy in your space.
I think the contract is that you can post messages in my space in return for
my right to do the same.

Spamming is the equivalent of walking around town and posting the same
message on every available bulletin board, etc.  It also the equivalent of
an organization like the Moonies or the Jehovah's Witnesses, or the Gideon's
sending people out to every street corner to spread the word.  I think
people have that right.  I'd rather they not exercise it.  It is annoying,
and it quickly becomes tiresome.  But, I would support their right to do so.

Spamming does not spill over into areas where you are not invited.  You do
not require an invitation to enter a public forum.  Anyone may freely enter
or leave.  A social contract based on the idea of invitation would allow for
censorship based on "appropriate venue".  I do not like that concept at all,
and for the reasons that Carl has stated so well.

As I think through this message, I do not believe that free expression is
based on a social contract that implies quid pro quo.  I believe the basis
of the contract is essentially Hobbesian.  We support free expression for
everyone because that is the only way we can guarantee it for ourselves.
If we make the right to express oneself subject to the amount of influence
someone has in society (how much power, how popular the idea, etc.), then
we all become vulnerable when society's values shift and we find ourselves
no longer standing with the majority.

Truth is not subject to a show of hands.  The fact that beliefs in society
change over time does not mean that what is true today is necessarily false
tomorrow.  People who think the new beliefs are wrong should have every
opportunity to persuade others.  The only way they can do that is if free
expression is guaranteed for all.


Mitch

