I agree with Carl about 85-90% of the time.  There are points where we
respectfully disagree.  If this is Carl's position, this is another
example where we disagree.  The same with JS Greenfield.  That's
perfectly okay.

I disagree that Usenet is a "limited public forum".  A "limited public
forum" is something like the common areas in a shopping mall (in some
states) or conference rooms at the public library or classrooms in a
public school.  The forum is limited because the primary purpose of the
space is not to be a public forum.  The primary purpose is to be a place
of commerce, a place to read and borrow books, or a place for educating
children.

That is not the case with Usenet.  Usenet is designed to facilitate the
public exchange of ideas.  Dissemination is, arguably, it primary
purpose.  Since that is what spammers do (and do very well), their
behavior is not inconsistent with the underlying rationale for Usenet.

> Under that doctrine, the administrators of the forum have the authority
