> the "reasonable venue" sobriquet.  (Obviously, they can already do this
> with any local newsgroups they may create.)
> 
Under that doctrine, private groups have the right to make use of public
facilities in order to further their own group interests.  They can, for
example, hold their meetings.  They can, for example, invite speakers.
They can even invite the public to join them.  But the forum itself remains
private.  The public is "invited" to join them.  That invitation can be
withdrawn at any point and for any reason.

The Usenet equivalent of the limited forum is the moderated newsgroup.
An even better equivalent is the list server.  With the list server, the
originator of the list decides whom to invite both to listen and to speak.
With the moderated newsgroup, the originator decides whom to invite to
speak (although has very limited control over who can listen).

Spamming is not an issue with moderated newsgroups.  Spammed messages
make it onto the moderated newsgroup only at the discretion of the
moderator.


