No, they are not free to do as they see fit.  Government agencies, by far
the most numerous sites, are not free to ignore state and U.S. constitutions.
They are not free to ignore statutory law.  Neither are individuals free to
ignore statutory law or to interfere with the exercise of civil rights
(not since the Civil Rights Act of 1964).


> In conclusion, I'm not convinced that your group can make a particularly
> strong stand against "reasonable venue" restrictions.
> 
I have never claimed that either position is a lead pipe cinch winner in
court.  There are enough legal issues in this question to keep many
lawyers and university administrators busy for a long time to come.

I count on two factors to aid advocates of unrestricted speech in this
coming battle.  First, university administrators are no different from
other people.  They have a marked preference for the path of least
resistance.  The more that an intolerant subset of users militate for
active enforcement of group charters, etc., the more the administrators
become aware of how costly meddling can become.  The intolerant, when they
are successful soon make more and more demands for the same action.  So,
starting down this path is a vicious cycle for system administrators.  As
the demands increase, so do challenges from spammers, flamers, etc.  Now
the administrators need to assign staff to study groups charters and
adjudicate.  Since computer centers are rarely over-funded, the demand
for those resources competes with other priorities.  Getting involved in
protracted legal battles costs universities lots of money and affects
their credibility as tolerant of all viewpoints.  Wise university
administrators would be very loathe to commit themselves to such a prospect.

I believe that the intolerant subset have had their way until now because
users have not been willing to mount a spirited defense of free expression.
I believe the trend will be in the direction of hands-off from system
administrators.  It is the only way to avoid being sucked into very
costly legal and public relations debacles.

As groups like the ACLU join into the fray, users will be able to
challenge the limits that the intolerant seek to impose on the net.



> >You can't get to the question of "appropriate venue" without passing through
