Again, an interesting point, Carl.

I would argue that it is not reasonable.

The purpose of Usenet is to facilitate the public exchange of viewpoints.
Imposing an arbitrary rule such as 25 newsgroups impedes the dissemination
of opinions.

Any limit such as 25 is also arbitrary.  I can think of many reasons why
someone might send the same message to more than 25 groups.  Perhaps one
wants to solicit information about cultural diversity for their research.
So they send a boiler-plate message to every soc.culture.xxxxx newsgroup.
Or they want to reach everyone interested in leisure-time activities.
So they send a boiler-plate request to every rec.xxxxx.xxxxx group and
many alt.xxxx.xxxx groups as well.  Now you are impeding serious discussions,
thereby violating the primary purpose of Usenet.

Moreover, being able to regulate time, place, and manner restrictions is not
carte blanche.  The restriction must be more than reasonable.  It must be
designed to further some legitimate interest of government or prevent
disruption of government activities.

I will stand by my claim in other messages.  When a subset of intolerant people 
try to suppress messages that annoy them, the result is greater inconvenience 
and annoyance for everyone.


