There is no evidence that anyone's property has been appropriated in any way,
let alone incompatible with the wishes of the owners.

The purpose of Usenet is to facilitate the public communication of ideas.
Spamming is wholly consistent with that purpose.  Spamming communicates
to a broader audience than the typical user, that's all.

Usenet incorporates cross-posting as a way to reach multiple audiences.
Spamming makes the ultimate use of that capability.

If a Usenet site does not want to carry certain newsgroups, they can easily
block them.  If they wish to block certain sites or individuals, that is
easy enough to accomplish as well.  If they wish to search for duplicate
messages in multiple newsgroups and delete them to recover disk space, that
can be done.  Therefore, there is no appropriation of someone else's
property.

When a site receives a Usenet feed, there is no guarantee that only messages
the system adminstrator approves of will come via that feed.  The receiving
site is responsible for any filtering, not the sending site.  There is no
mechanism for the sending site to know what are the wishes of the receiving
site.

What is preposterous is the notion that spamming messages is nothing more
than cross-posting taken to its extreme.  There is no way to devise a clear
and unambiguous rule for distinguishing between spamming and cross-posting.
There are many perfectly acceptable reasons why someone might send a message
to hundreds of Usenet groups.  Social scientists doing research might want
send a brief request for information to all of the soc.culture groups.
Researchers interested in recreation might send short messages to all of
the rec. groups and even many of the alt. groups and comp. groups.  I fail
to see how such use of cross-posting is inconsistent with the purposes of
Usenet.


Mitch

