Let's say that I as a state university administrator initially set up a
full feed for my site because we have lots of disk space.  Usenet traffic
is always growing, and eventually starts to bump up against my disk
capacity.  I request funds to buy more disks to maintain a full feed and
am turned down.  I have a couple of options open to me.  I can lower
expiry times or I can drop some groups.  I decide to pitch the binaries
groups because they are huge space hogs (which is supposedly okay with
your).  I lower expiry times to 3 days from 7 on some other high-volume
groups (which is also probably okay with you).  Eventually Usenet traffic
gets so high that I am faced with having to cut more groups.  If I am
bound by the "no looking at content, only volume" rule, then I am probably
required to nuke comp.lang.c and leave alt.bonehead.andrew-beckwith and
alt.fan.john-palmer and alt.enya.puke.puke.puke, ad infinitum.  Eventually,
I am left with a bunch of relatively low-volume groups that have very little
to do with the academic needs of the university.  I might as well drop
Usenet altogether.  

