A brief comment here:

gmitchel is falling into what is known as a 'fallacy of composition'.
That is, the argument goes, 'the purpose of Usenet is to facilitate
the public exchange of viewpoints,' therefore the purpose of any and
all parts of Usenet is to facilitate the public exchange of viewpoints.

A similar argument could be made thusly:  the purpose of a university
is to provide education, therefore I can lecture at any time, in any
place at a university, and on any subject, as I am, by lecturing,
contributing to the purpose of the university.  This example makes
clear, I think, why this type of argument is a fallacy.

It is of course true that a public university in the United States
(things may be different elsewhere) cannot absolutely prohibit me
from lecturing.  Nonetheless, there are certain times and places
where I have no right to speak, and others where my speaking may
be required to be 'on topic.'  If I refuse to go along with these
restrictions, I may be removed.

Extension of the above paragraph to Usenet is left as an exercise
for the reader.

