Is there anything immoral about assigning each site administrator the
responsibility of policing his own site?  It seems to me that this is
inherent in the job of a site administrator.  In general, nobody *can*
take action directly against an offending individual, save only the
site administrator; so clearly the responsibility can reside nowhere else.

This means that if a problem persists, it is because a site administrator
is not doing his job.  The only way the net populace has to discipline
the site administrator is via masses of mail sufficient to cause him
problems with his systems. 

It doesn't matter whether the mail is addressed to the administrator, or
to the user, as far as this is concerned.  At lower levels there are
advantages to either destination.  Sometimes a user will be concerned
about his own offences and try to stop; sometimes a user won't.  Sometimes
a site administrator will be concerned about offenses by his users and
try to stop them; sometimes he won't.  *One* note has to get to somebody
who gives a shit; several thousand notes will compel someone (the site
administrator) to care.

If anyone thinks this kind of response is wrong... tell us what other means
exist to deal with troublemakers.

